<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <AssemblyName>MartenStudio</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>MartenStudio</RootNamespace>
    <PackageId>MartenStudio</PackageId>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageTags>marten;martendb;postgresql;event-sourcing;document-database;admin;dashboard;ui;blazor;aspnetcore</PackageTags>
    <Description>An embeddable Blazor Server admin UI for MartenDB. Register it with AddMartenStudio() and mount it with MapMartenStudio() inside your own ASP.NET Core application; it runs against that application's own configured IDocumentStore. Browse document collections and JSON, inspect and steer event streams, projections and the async daemon, run guarded queries and manage schema. Every mutating operation is an individual capability flag and the studio refuses to start without an explicit authorization decision.</Description>
    <EnablePackageValidation>true</EnablePackageValidation>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Not trimmable, said out loud rather than left to the default. Blazor Server is a reflective
    framework by construction: a component's [Parameter] properties are set by name from the render
    tree, and the document viewer deserializes into types discovered from IDocumentType.DocumentType
    at run time. Marking this IsTrimmable would tell a trimmer it may cut into a graph nothing
    statically references. README.md says the same for anyone reading on nuget.org.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsTrimmable>false</IsTrimmable>
    <IsAotCompatible>false</IsAotCompatible>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The package tells its consumer that it needs the ASP.NET Core web-assets pack, because the consumer
    has no way of knowing: see the long comment in MartenStudio.build.props. The Static Web Assets SDK
    generates build/$(PackageId).props itself and offers exactly one way to put something else in it -
    turn that generation off and pack your own file that imports what the generated one imported. Only
    the build/ entry point is replaced; the SDK still generates buildMultiTargeting/ and
    buildTransitive/, and both chain into ours.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <StaticWebAssetsDisableProjectBuildPropsFileGeneration>true</StaticWebAssetsDisableProjectBuildPropsFileGeneration>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="../../README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" />
    <None Update="MartenStudio.build.props" Pack="true" PackagePath="build/MartenStudio.props" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The studio ships its own copy of blazor.web.js.

    As of .NET 10 that file is not in the shared framework and not embedded in any ASP.NET Core assembly
    (checked: Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Server/Web/Endpoints carry no manifest resources). It comes
    from the Microsoft.AspNetCore.App.Internal.Assets pack, which the SDK resolves only for a project
    whose `RequiresAspNetWebAssets` is true - and the Web SDK defaults that to true only for a project
    that has `.razor` Content items of its own. A host that adds Marten Studio and writes no Blazor
    components has none, so `GET /_framework/blazor.web.js` is a 404, the studio prerenders, never becomes
    interactive, and nothing says a word.

    The package cannot set the property on the consumer's behalf. It is a restore-time decision - the
    implicit reference to the assets pack is added while the restore graph is built - and NuGet
    deliberately excludes package-provided .props/.targets from restore evaluation
    (ExcludeRestorePackageImports). Measured, not assumed: with the property set from a consumer's own
    Directory.Build.props the pack appears in project.assets.json and blazor.web.js appears in the
    consumer's static web asset manifest; set from this package's build/MartenStudio.props it does not,
    even after a forced restore. The props file still ships, because the property is also read at
    build time and costs nothing to state early, but it is not what makes this work.

    So this project sets `RequiresAspNetWebAssets` for ITSELF (restore does evaluate a project's own
    csproj) and republishes the one file out of the resolved pack as a static web asset of its own, at
    `_content/MartenStudio/_framework/blazor.web.js`. The mapped `{Path}/_framework/blazor.web.js`
    endpoint prefers whatever the host serves and falls back to this copy, so a host that did configure
    web assets is unaffected and a host that did not gets a working studio instead of a silent one.

    THE RISK, said out loud: this couples the shipped script to the ASP.NET Core patch version bundled
    with the SDK that built the package. The negotiated Blazor protocol is stable across patches and the
    host's own copy always wins when there is one, so the exposure is a host with no web assets of its own
    running a much newer runtime than the package was built against. The clean fix is a PackageReference
    to Microsoft.AspNetCore.App.Internal.Assets, whose buildTransitive targets would define the asset in
    the CONSUMER at the consumer's own version - that is a package-budget decision (AGENTS.md hard rule 1)
    and is recorded as a follow-up rather than taken here.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RequiresAspNetWebAssets>true</RequiresAspNetWebAssets>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Declared the way the assets pack declares it for an application (see
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.App.Internal.Assets.targets): hook ResolveStaticWebAssetsInputs and call the
    SDK's own DefineStaticWebAssets / DefineStaticWebAssetEndpoints. A Content item with a Link into
    wwwroot does not work - the Razor SDK discovers project assets by their path on disk, and this file
    is in the NuGet cache. AssetMode="All" so the asset reaches referencing projects and the package
    alike; no fingerprint, because the studio's own endpoint resolves it by its logical path.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ResolveStaticWebAssetsInputsDependsOn>
      $(ResolveStaticWebAssetsInputsDependsOn);
      MartenStudioAddBlazorWebScript;
    </ResolveStaticWebAssetsInputsDependsOn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Target Name="MartenStudioAddBlazorWebScript">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <!--
        Copied into obj/ before it is declared, rather than declared where the pack put it. A consuming
        application that resolves the same pack (any host with .razor files of its own) would otherwise
        see the identical file path arrive twice - once as its own framework asset, once as ours - and
        DiscoverPrecompressedAssets fails the build on the duplicate key. Same bytes, distinct identity.
        Computed in the target rather than in a PropertyGroup above, because IntermediateOutputPath is
        not set until the common targets have been imported.
      -->
      <MartenStudioFrameworkAssetRoot>$([MSBuild]::NormalizeDirectory('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)', '$(IntermediateOutputPath)', 'martenstudio-framework'))</MartenStudioFrameworkAssetRoot>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <Error Condition="!Exists('$(BlazorFrameworkStaticWebAssetRoot)\blazor.web.js')"
           Text="Marten Studio ships its own copy of blazor.web.js and could not find one at '$(BlazorFrameworkStaticWebAssetRoot)\blazor.web.js'. That path comes from the Microsoft.AspNetCore.App.Internal.Assets pack, which this project resolves by setting RequiresAspNetWebAssets; a restore that did not bring it in would leave hosts with no Blazor script and a studio that silently never becomes interactive, so the build stops here instead." />

    <Copy SourceFiles="$(BlazorFrameworkStaticWebAssetRoot)\blazor.web.js"
          DestinationFolder="$(MartenStudioFrameworkAssetRoot)"
          SkipUnchangedFiles="true">
      <Output TaskParameter="DestinationFiles" ItemName="FileWrites" />
    </Copy>

    <ItemGroup>
      <MartenStudioBlazorScriptCandidate Include="$(MartenStudioFrameworkAssetRoot)blazor.web.js">
        <RelativePath>_framework/blazor.web.js</RelativePath>
      </MartenStudioBlazorScriptCandidate>
    </ItemGroup>

    <DefineStaticWebAssets CandidateAssets="@(MartenStudioBlazorScriptCandidate)"
                           ContentRoot="$(MartenStudioFrameworkAssetRoot)"
                           SourceId="$(PackageId)"
                           SourceType="Discovered"
                           AssetKind="All"
                           AssetMode="All"
                           AssetRole="Primary"
                           FingerprintCandidates="false"
                           BasePath="$(StaticWebAssetBasePath)">
      <Output TaskParameter="Assets" ItemName="MartenStudioBlazorScriptAsset" />
    </DefineStaticWebAssets>

    <DefineStaticWebAssetEndpoints CandidateAssets="@(MartenStudioBlazorScriptAsset)"
                                   ExistingEndpoints=""
                                   ContentTypeMappings="@(StaticWebAssetContentTypeMapping)">
      <Output TaskParameter="Endpoints" ItemName="MartenStudioBlazorScriptEndpoint" />
    </DefineStaticWebAssetEndpoints>

    <ItemGroup>
      <StaticWebAsset Include="@(MartenStudioBlazorScriptAsset)" />
      <StaticWebAssetEndpoint Include="@(MartenStudioBlazorScriptEndpoint)" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Blazor Server, SignalR, authorization and the endpoint routing the studio mounts into all
         come from the shared framework, so the package pins no ASP.NET Core patch on its host. -->
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
    <PackageReference Include="Marten" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <SupportedPlatform Include="browser" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="MartenStudio.Tests" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="MartenStudio.Integration.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
